PLATFORM NOTES
Friday, March 20, 2026

Why incumbents ship 'lite' versions

A pattern from this month's trade shows: the large enterprise vendors in manufacturing software are announcing "lite" scheduling, "lite" analytics, "lite" quality modules, bundled free or nearly free with the core license. Startups in those categories are alarmed. They should be attentive rather than alarmed, because the lite module is a specific kind of move with a specific kind of failure.

The incumbent ships lite for two reasons. It wants to remove the budget line the startup would have used, and it wants to buy time until it can acquire or build the real thing. Lite is a placeholder with a marketing budget.

Lite fails, when it fails, for reasons that are also predictable. It is built by a team that does not own the outcome, so it is never quite finished. It is configured by the customer's IT department rather than by the operations team that would use it, so it is never quite right. And it is free, so nobody inside the customer is accountable for making it work. Two years later the plant is still running the spreadsheet, the lite module is still "being rolled out," and the startup's salesperson is back in the building.

The startup's correct response is not to compete on features, which the incumbent will match on slides. It is to compete on deployment: how fast a plant gets to a working schedule, how many of the plant's own rules it captures, and how many weeks the startup's people spend on site making it true. Lite cannot do that, because lite does not have people.

